
## Patient–Ventilator Asynchrony (PVA): Diagnosis and Management

Patient–ventilator asynchrony (PVA) is a mismatch between the patient's neural respiratory drive (timing, flow, volume, pressure demands) and the ventilator's delivered support during mechanical ventilation. [^1_1][^1_2][^1_3] It is common (reported in 10–85% of ventilated patients depending on definitions and monitoring) and is associated with increased work of breathing, discomfort, prolonged ventilation, higher tracheostomy rates, and in some studies increased mortality. [^1_4][^1_5][^1_6]

Below is an exam‑style, high‑yield note structured for quick recall and bedside use.

***

## 1. Definition and Clinical Relevance

- **Definition**: PVA = discordance between patient's inspiratory/expiratory neural timing or demand and ventilator's trigger, flow delivery, cycling, or pressure/volume provision. [^1_1][^1_2]
- **Why it matters**:
    - Increases **work of breathing** and dyspnea. [^1_1][^1_7]
    - Can cause **ventilator-induced lung injury (VILI)** via breath stacking, high tidal volumes, or excessive transpulmonary pressures. [^1_1][^1_8]
    - Associated with **longer duration of MV**, more tracheostomies, and in some cohorts higher ICU/hospital mortality. [^1_4][^1_5][^1_6]
    - May reflect **disease severity** as well as suboptimal ventilator settings or sedation. [^1_5][^1_6]

***

## 2. Classification of Asynchronies (by Respiratory Phase)

A practical classification aligns with the **four phases** of the breath: trigger → flow delivery → cycling → expiration. [^1_1][^1_9][^1_7]


| Phase | Asynchrony Type | Core Mechanism |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Trigger** | Ineffective effort (missed trigger) | Patient effort fails to trigger ventilator |
|  | Trigger delay | Marked delay between effort onset and ventilator trigger |
|  | Auto-triggering | Ventilator triggers without patient effort |
|  | Reverse triggering | Ventilator breath triggers patient effort (in controlled modes) |
| **Flow delivery** | Flow starvation / inadequate flow | Delivered flow < patient demand |
|  | Excessive flow | Flow too high for patient comfort/demand |
| **Cycling (inspiratory→expiratory)** | Premature (short) cycling | Ventilator ends inspiration before patient neural inspiration ends |
|  | Delayed (prolonged) cycling | Ventilator continues inspiration after patient neural inspiration ends |
| **Expiratory/Other** | Double triggering / breath stacking | Two ventilator cycles for one neural effort (often due to short cycling or high drive) |
|  | Expiratory muscle activation against ventilator | Active expiration during mechanical insufflation |

Seven commonly cited types: ineffective effort, double triggering, premature cycling, delayed cycling, reverse triggering, flow starvation, and auto-triggering. [^1_1][^1_9]

***

## 3. Pathophysiology and Predisposing Factors

PVA arises from imbalance among:

- **Patient factors**:
    - High respiratory drive: pain, anxiety, hypoxemia, hypercapnia, acidosis, fever, sepsis, ARDS. [^1_4][^1_8]
    - Low respiratory drive: deep sedation, neuromuscular weakness, metabolic derangements. [^1_1]
    - Altered mechanics: COPD/asthma (high resistance, PEEPi), ARDS (low compliance), obesity, chest wall restriction, pneumothorax, secretions/obstruction. [^1_10][^1_8]
- **Ventilator factors**:
    - Inappropriate trigger sensitivity (too low → missed triggers; too high → auto-triggering). [^1_1][^1_11]
    - Inadequate inspiratory flow or rise time. [^1_1][^1_8]
    - Mismatched inspiratory time / cycling criteria (flow % in PSV, fixed Ti in PCV/VCV). [^1_1][^1_7]
    - Leaks (especially in NIV), condensation in circuit, cardiogenic oscillations causing auto-triggering. [^1_1][^1_11][^1_12]
- **Interface and airway**:
    - Endotracheal tube issues (kinking, mucus plug, cuff leak), bronchospasm, dynamic hyperinflation. [^1_10][^1_11]

Conceptually, asynchronies cluster into:

- **High drive asynchronies**: flow starvation, premature cycling, double triggering (often need more support or treat drive). [^1_1]
- **Low drive asynchronies**: ineffective efforts, delayed cycling, reverse triggering (often due to over‑assistance or sedation). [^1_1]

